  Careful with `command.execute(parsed.args, ctx)` — the union type `AnyGateCommand` has `parse(args: string): CommandParse<A>` for different A, and `execute(args: A, ctx)`. TypeScript with a union of function types: calling `command.parse(rest)` on a union type where the signatures differ... The union's call signature is an intersection of parameters — for `parse(args: string): CommandParse<{prompt?:string}> | CommandParse<{steps:number}> | CommandParse<undefined>`, calling with a string arg should work since all accept one string param. But the return type will be a union of CommandParse types, and `parsed.args` will be a union — calling `command.execute(parsed.args, ctx)` on a union of execute signatures is problematic in TS (union of function types can only be called if parameters are compatible; TS 3.3+ supports calling a union of functions with the intersection of parameters).

  The execute signatures: `(args: {prompt?:string}, ctx) => void` | `(args: {steps:number}, ctx) => void` | `(args: undefined, ctx) => void`. Calling with a union argument `{prompt?:string} | {steps:number} | undefined` → TS will complain: "Each member of the union has signatures, but none of those signatures are compatible with each other" — actually TS 3.3+ allows calling a union of function types when the parameters can be intersected; the argument type becomes the intersection of parameter types, which for these would be `({prompt?:string} & {steps:number} & undefined)` = never. So it errors.

