  Hmm, `command.execute(parsed.args, ctx)` — TypeScript: `AnyGateCommand` is a union of GateCommand<A> with different A; calling execute(parsed.args...) on a union is a TS problem ("not callable, signatures are not compatible"). The union's execute param types differ ({prompt?}|{steps}|undefined). TS will complain.

  Workaround: type the registry as a common shape. Since `AnyGateCommand` is a union, calling `command.parse(rest)` also has issues: parse returns CommandParse<A> with different A → the union of return types. Calling parse on a union of function types: TS 3.3+ supports calling a union of signatures with the intersection of parameters — the parameter is `string` for all → OK. The return type is a union of CommandParse<{prompt?}> | CommandParse<{steps}> | CommandParse<undefined> → accessing `.args` gives a union; `.ok` narrowing works.

  But `execute(parsed.args, ctx)`: parsed.args is a union type; execute expects the specific A. TS will error: "Each member of the union has signatures, but none of those signatures are compatible with each other."

  Solution: cast through a local erased type:
  ```ts
  type ErasedGateCommand = {
  	parse(args: string): { ok: true; args: unknown } | { ok: false; error: CommandError };
  	execute(args: never, ctx: CommandContext): void;
  };
  ```
